WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.215
Lesson 1.9.

00:00:02.215 --> 00:00:04.760
Smart accounts and account abstraction.

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:07.081
By the end of this lesson, you'll be able

00:00:07.081 --> 00:00:10.452
to understand how smart accounts change wallet security, and

00:00:10.452 --> 00:00:12.883
use their features without adding new risks.

00:00:14.183 --> 00:00:15.473
Here's the plan.

00:00:15.473 --> 00:00:17.158
First, the key ideas.

00:00:17.158 --> 00:00:19.873
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:19.873 --> 00:00:21.398
Then, your checklist.

00:00:21.398 --> 00:00:23.560
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:28.029
First, A normal wallet (an EOA, externally owned

00:00:28.029 --> 00:00:31.045
account) is controlled by one private key.

00:00:31.045 --> 00:00:32.715
A smart account is a contract

00:00:32.715 --> 00:00:34.685
wallet whose rules you choose.

00:00:34.685 --> 00:00:38.811
Next, ERC-4337 (account abstraction): smart

00:00:38.811 --> 00:00:43.050
accounts without changing Ethereum itself.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:48.830
Transactions become "user operations" handled by bundlers; paymasters

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:52.020
can pay gas for you (e.g. in USDC).

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:57.018
Then, Features: multisig and social recovery (trusted "guardians" can

00:00:57.018 --> 00:01:02.086
restore access), spending limits, session keys (Lesson 14.2), batching

00:01:02.086 --> 00:01:05.442
(approve + swap in one step), passkeys (log in

00:01:05.442 --> 00:01:09.740
with your device's biometrics instead of a seed phrase).

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:14.095
Also, EIP-7702 (2025): lets an ordinary wallet

00:01:14.095 --> 00:01:19.561
temporarily or permanently delegate to smart-account code:

00:01:19.561 --> 00:01:24.030
powerful, and a phishing target (Lesson 1.7).

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:28.462
And finally, New risks: the account's contract code, module/plugin

00:01:28.462 --> 00:01:33.356
permissions, guardian collusion, and differences between chains (the same

00:01:33.356 --> 00:01:35.870
address may not exist on every chain).

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:40.185
Let's look at the diagram: The 3-wallet setup.

00:01:40.185 --> 00:01:41.745
Take a moment with it.

00:01:41.745 --> 00:01:44.155
It's everything we've just said, in one picture.

00:01:45.455 --> 00:01:47.970
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:51.819
Your operating wallet becomes a smart account with: a

00:01:51.819 --> 00:01:55.383
passkey on your phone plus a hardware key (2-of-2

00:01:55.383 --> 00:01:59.162
for large moves); a $2,000/day limit for small moves

00:01:59.162 --> 00:02:02.868
with just the passkey; 3 guardians (2-of-3) who can

00:02:02.868 --> 00:02:06.875
recover access after a 48-hour delay you can cancel.

00:02:06.875 --> 00:02:09.775
Lose your phone: recover via guardians.

00:02:09.775 --> 00:02:12.452
Phone stolen: the thief is capped at

00:02:12.452 --> 00:02:15.057
$2,000/day and you have 48 hours to

00:02:15.057 --> 00:02:17.590
cancel a recovery you didn't start.

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:20.350
Here's your checklist.

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:21.885
Do it now, for real.

00:02:21.885 --> 00:02:23.873
First, I know which of my wallets

00:02:23.873 --> 00:02:26.395
are EOAs and which are smart accounts.

00:02:26.395 --> 00:02:28.955
Next, Guardians chosen so no two could

00:02:28.955 --> 00:02:32.340
plausibly collude; recovery has a cancel delay.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:36.710
Then, Module/plugin permissions reviewed like approvals.

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:39.999
And finally, Smart account deployed (or deployable)

00:02:39.999 --> 00:02:41.580
on every chain I send to.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:44.090
Question 1.

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:45.425
What does a paymaster do?

00:02:49.845 --> 00:02:52.878
The answer: Pays gas on your behalf,

00:02:52.878 --> 00:02:55.665
e.g. letting you pay fees in USDC.

00:02:56.965 --> 00:02:58.210
Question 2.

00:02:58.210 --> 00:02:59.625
What is social recovery?

00:03:04.045 --> 00:03:07.119
The answer: Trusted guardians can restore access

00:03:07.119 --> 00:03:09.565
to your account, usually after a delay.

00:03:10.865 --> 00:03:12.135
Question 3.

00:03:12.135 --> 00:03:13.785
Why check the smart account

00:03:13.785 --> 00:03:15.670
exists on the destination chain?

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:22.917
The answer: Smart-account addresses may not be

00:03:22.917 --> 00:03:25.444
deployed on every chain; funds sent there

00:03:25.444 --> 00:03:26.767
may be hard to access.

00:03:28.068 --> 00:03:29.313
Let's recap.

00:03:29.313 --> 00:03:31.948
A normal wallet (an EOA, externally owned

00:03:31.948 --> 00:03:34.883
account) is controlled by one private key.

00:03:34.883 --> 00:03:42.423
ERC-4337 (account abstraction): smart accounts without changing Ethereum itself.

00:03:42.423 --> 00:03:45.592
Features: multisig and social recovery (trusted

00:03:45.592 --> 00:03:49.128
"guardians" can restore access), spending limits.

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:54.117
EIP-7702 (2025): lets an ordinary wallet temporarily

00:03:54.117 --> 00:03:58.448
or permanently delegate to smart-account code.

00:03:59.748 --> 00:04:02.263
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:04:02.263 --> 00:04:05.408
Next up, Lesson 2.0: Mastery Starter.
